\section*{Triangular-cylinder approximation of aligned radial sweeps}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), the asserted result is that radial segment sweeps have finite triangular-base cylinder realizations whose area converges to the weighted parameter cost, including a tetrahedral cover below the half-area bound.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Choose a Euclidean plane $P$ with unit normal $e$, independent $a,b\in P$, $J=[\alpha,\beta]$ with $\alpha<\beta$, continuous $R\ge0$, a $C^1$ map $w:J\to P$, $p_0\in P$ and $L>0$. Set
\[
 \mathcal S=\{p_0+\rho(a+tb)+s(e+w(t)):t\in J,0\le\rho\le R(t),|s|\le L\},
 \quad\mathcal A=|\det_P(a,b)|\int_{\alpha}^{\beta}\frac{R(t)^2}{2\sqrt{1+\|w(t)\|^2}}\,dt.
\]
If $w'(t)=\lambda(t)(a+tb)$ for continuous real $\lambda$, every tagged finite partition gives one covering cylinder per interval, with frozen axis $e+w(c_i)$ and nondegenerate compact triangular perpendicular base, whose total base area tends uniformly to $\mathcal A$ as the mesh tends to zero. Frozen projections of the actual interval sweeps give the same limit. The tetrahedral consequence claims, for each regular tetrahedron $K$ of positive edge length, a finite triangular-base cover with
\[
 \sum_i|B_i|<\tfrac12 A_{\min}(K),\qquad
 \sum_i\frac{|B_i|}{|\pi_{u_i^{\perp}}K|}<\tfrac12.
\]

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), one triangular enclosure is assigned to each interval of a tagged partition. Complementary shortened beams leave a strip covered by horizontal cylinders; exact overlap and strip calculations cancel the first-order cost and leave a strict second-order saving.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The construction concerns aligned radial sweeps, tagged partitions, and convergence of perpendicular-base area to the weighted parameter cost. Complete coverage of the displayed approximation statement is not established by this catalogue, so its claim status remains partial.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: radial segment sweeps have finite triangular-base cylinder realizations whose area converges to the weighted parameter cost, including a tetrahedral cover below the half-area bound.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CylinderCovering.lean}, \texttt{RuledCovering.lean}, \texttt{TriangularCovering.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CylinderCovering.fourPaperMain}, \texttt{OAI.RuledApproximation.fullMain}, \texttt{OAI.TriangularCovering.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

